A run may cross a block group here

lwext4 warned when a range being freed spanned two block groups without
flex_bg, on the reasoning that a group begins with its own bitmaps and
inode table so no contiguous run of data blocks can reach into the next
one. That is true of mke2fs, which puts the block bitmap at the first
block of any group carrying no superblock backup. It is not true of the
volumes this driver formats: every group leaves three blocks free at its
head, backup or not, and the allocator hands them out like any others.

Measured rather than argued. On a 256 MB volume with 1 KiB blocks an
extent runs from block 96318 for 1990 blocks, three blocks into group
12; those three hold file data, e2fsck reports no errors, and the loop
directly below the warning already walks bg_first..bg_last and frees
each group's share correctly. The only thing wrong was the diagnostic.

It had never been heard, for two compounding reasons. ext4_dbg compiled
to nothing until 0059, and a run has to be long to reach a group
boundary at all -- one write call's worth never does. Reserving space
ahead of a write made runs long enough, and the first thing the newly
audible channel said was this.

That sequencing is worth keeping in view: enforcement now depends on
this channel being quiet (patch 0060 refuses writes through a bitmap
whose checksum fails), so a false warning is not cosmetic. Every suite
is silent again with it gone.

--- a/src/ext4_balloc.c
+++ b/src/ext4_balloc.c
@@ -300,15 +300,28 @@
 	/* Compute indexes */
 	uint32_t bg_last = ext4_balloc_get_bgid_of_block(sb, first + count - 1);
 
-	if (!ext4_sb_feature_incom(sb, EXT4_FINCOM_FLEX_BG)) {
-		/*It is not possible without flex_bg that blocks are continuous
-		 * and and last block belongs to other bg.*/
-		if (bg_last != bg_first) {
-			ext4_dbg(DEBUG_BALLOC, DBG_WARN "FLEX_BG: disabled & "
-				"bg_last: %"PRIu32" bg_first: %"PRIu32"\n",
-				bg_last, bg_first);
-		}
-	}
+	/*
+	 * A range that crosses a block group is normal here, and the warning
+	 * that used to stand in this spot was wrong.
+	 *
+	 * Its premise was that without flex_bg a group begins with its own
+	 * bitmaps and inode table, so no contiguous run of data blocks can
+	 * reach into the next group. That holds for mke2fs, which puts the
+	 * block bitmap at the first block of a group that carries no
+	 * superblock backup. It does not hold for the volumes this driver
+	 * formats: every group leaves three blocks free at its head, backup or
+	 * not, and the allocator hands them out like any others.
+	 *
+	 * Measured rather than assumed. On a 256 MB volume with 1 KiB blocks,
+	 * an extent runs from block 96318 for 1990 blocks, three blocks into
+	 * group 12 -- and those three hold file data, e2fsck reports no
+	 * errors, and the loop below already walks bg_first..bg_last freeing
+	 * each group's share. The only thing wrong was the diagnostic.
+	 *
+	 * It stayed invisible until writes began reserving space ahead of
+	 * themselves, because a run has to be long to reach a boundary at all.
+	 * Patch 0059 is what made it audible, which is the point of 0059.
+	 */
 
 	/* Load block group reference */
 	struct ext4_block_group_ref bg_ref;
